Lab 11: Start and Stop the Overcloud
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Manage the lifecycle of an OpenStack overcloud, including starting and stopping services.
Perform graceful shutdowns and rolling restarts of overcloud nodes.
Automate start/stop sequences using orchestration tools like Ansible.
Prerequisites
A deployed OpenStack overcloud (controller, compute, network, and storage nodes).
SSH access to the undercloud node with stack user privileges.
Basic familiarity with OpenStack CLI and Ansible.
Installed openstack-client and ansible packages.
Task 1: Start Overcloud Services
Subtask 1.1: Verify Overcloud Status
Before starting services, check the current state of the overcloud.

source ~/stackrc  # Load undercloud environment
openstack stack list  # Verify overcloud stack status
openstack server list  # List all overcloud nodes
Expected Outcome:

The openstack stack list should show the overcloud stack as CREATE_COMPLETE or UPDATE_COMPLETE.
openstack server list should display all nodes (controller, compute, etc.) with ACTIVE status.
Subtask 1.2: Start All Overcloud Nodes
If nodes are stopped, start them using the following commands:

# Start controller nodes
for node in $(openstack server list --name controller -f value -c ID); do
    openstack server start $node
done

# Start compute nodes
for node in $(openstack server list --name compute -f value -c ID); do
    openstack server start $node
done
Expected Outcome:

Nodes transition from SHUTOFF to ACTIVE status. Verify using:
openstack server list
Troubleshooting:

If nodes fail to start, check hypervisor resources (e.g., RAM/CPU) with:
openstack hypervisor stats show
Task 2: Graceful Shutdown and Rolling Restarts
Subtask 2.1: Graceful Shutdown of Services
To avoid data corruption, gracefully shut down services on controller nodes:

# SSH into a controller node
ssh heat-admin@<controller-ip>

# Stop all OpenStack services gracefully
sudo systemctl stop 'openstack-*'
sudo systemctl stop 'neutron-*'
sudo systemctl stop 'httpd'
Expected Outcome:

Services stop without errors. Verify with:
sudo systemctl list-units --all | grep openstack
Subtask 2.2: Rolling Restart of Compute Nodes
Minimize downtime by restarting compute nodes one at a time:

# Disable a compute node
openstack compute service set <hostname> nova-compute --disable

# Restart the node
openstack server reboot <compute-node-id> --soft  # Soft reboot (graceful)

# Re-enable the node after restart
openstack compute service set <hostname> nova-compute --enable
Expected Outcome:

Compute nodes restart without affecting running VMs (live migration if enabled).
Task 3: Automate Start/Stop with Ansible
Subtask 3.1: Create an Ansible Playbook
Automate overcloud start/stop with an Ansible playbook (overcloud-management.yml):

---
- name: Manage Overcloud Lifecycle
  hosts: localhost
  tasks:
    - name: Start all overcloud nodes
      command: "openstack server start {{ item }}"
      loop: "{{ lookup('pipe', 'openstack server list -f value -c ID') }}"
      when: action == 'start'

    - name: Stop all overcloud nodes
      command: "openstack server stop {{ item }}"
      loop: "{{ lookup('pipe', 'openstack server list -f value -c ID') }}"
      when: action == 'stop'
Subtask 3.2: Execute the Playbook
Run the playbook to start/stop nodes:

# Start all nodes
ansible-playbook overcloud-management.yml -e "action=start"

# Stop all nodes
ansible-playbook overcloud-management.yml -e "action=stop"
Expected Outcome:

Playbook executes without errors, and nodes change state as specified.
Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Started overcloud services and verified their status.
Performed graceful shutdowns and rolling restarts to minimize downtime.
Automated lifecycle management using Ansible.
Key Takeaways:

Always verify node status before making changes.
Use rolling restarts for high-availability environments.
Automation reduces human error in repetitive tasks.
